# larkdocio — example environment file.
#
# Copy this to `.env` in the directory you run larkdocio from, then fill in real
# values. `.env` is gitignored and MUST NEVER be committed (only this .example is
# tracked). See README § "Credentials & .env".
#
# Notes below that name a path under dev/, requirements/ or process/, or the
# documents PROJECT.md and CLAUDE.md, cite the maintainers' own records, which are
# kept in their private working repository and are not part of the public one;
# each note states the fact it needs, so nothing here depends on opening them.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # then edit .env
#
# You can also point at an .env elsewhere (note --env-file is a GLOBAL option and
# must come BEFORE the subcommand):
#   larkdocio --env-file /path/to/.env download "<URL>" -o ./out/

# WHICH SCOPES, AUTHORITATIVELY: the scope names below are a convenience copy for
# setting the app up. The single source is the library's own permission registry
# (`larkdocio.permissions`), projected per operation into `larkdocio.capabilities()`
# and, for a human, into the permissions section of `larkdocio.guide()`.

# ── Read credentials (READ app — the reader, the shipped surface) ────────────
# The READ app pair. Used when BOTH are set. This app needs:
#   * Scope `docx:document:readonly` — download docs/blocks (the core reader).
#     Without it nothing can be read at all.
#   * Scope `bitable:app:readonly` — read a Base (app metadata, tables, views,
#     fields, records). Without it no Base can be read at all: every Base call
#     fails, including the embedded-Base deep read inside a doc.
#   * Scope `directory:employee:search` — turn a person's NAME into an open_id
#     (the contacts lookup surface). Without it a name cannot be turned into an
#     open_id at all: the call is denied outright and the lookup raises rather
#     than reporting the person as absent. There is no degrade — this library
#     will not enumerate the directory to approximate a name search.
#   * Read access to each target document (doc "…" menu → Add Document App), or
#     membership of the wiki space for wiki URLs.
#   * OPTIONAL: scope `drive:export` — the Sheets whole-cell style and
#     number-format read. Without it that read degrades to values only and
#     nothing else changes.
#   * A CANDIDATE, not a confirmed requirement — do NOT grant it on this file's
#     word alone: `board:whiteboard:node:read` is carried in this project's own
#     prose for the whiteboard/board image download and is labelled
#     documented-unverified, because Lark has never been observed demanding it
#     (the download succeeds on the granted set above). Try it only if that
#     download is denied, and read the denial body — Lark's own denial names
#     what it actually wants, and that is the only thing that establishes a
#     requirement.
#   * OPTIONAL, for default @mention resolution (resolve_mentions=true, LD-053):
#     TWO contacts scopes, needed TOGETHER —
#       - `contact:contact.base:readonly` (exact string from Lark's own error
#         body) opens the directory call at all;
#       - `contact:user.base:readonly` is the FIELD-level grant that makes the
#         name fields non-null. With the first alone the call SUCCEEDS and every
#         name comes back null, which is indistinguishable from "no such user" —
#         this is the misdiagnosis LD-173 records (the granted range was never
#         the gate; the missing field scope was).
#     — AND the app's contact-data VISIBLE RANGE must cover the mentioned users
#     (a separate console setting from app availability). Users outside the range,
#     or a missing scope, resolve to the raw `@ou_id` — the run GRACEFULLY
#     DEGRADES per user after one info log; it never errors.
#     Set resolve_mentions=false in config.json to skip resolution entirely.
#     In-process reads resolve only when the parser is handed the read client:
#     `Parser(Config(), read_client=client)` (the CLI does this for you).
LARK_READ_APP_ID=
LARK_READ_APP_SECRET=

# Legacy fallback: used only when the READ pair above is not fully set. Every
# existing .env keeps working. `larkdocio config --appId … --appSecret …` writes
# the preferred LARK_READ_APP_ID / LARK_READ_APP_SECRET above; on a .env that
# already uses the two names below it updates THOSE in place (it never re-keys a
# credential file for you) and says so.
# LARK_APP_ID=
# LARK_APP_SECRET=

# ── Write credentials (WRITE app — the writer, W-ladder work) ─────────────────
# SEPARATE, narrowly-scoped credentials — ideally a second app (a project
# decision from the writer's first milestone; one app or two, see below) —
# NEVER consulted by the read path (least-privilege read/write separation;
# enforced: the reader resolves only the READ/legacy pairs above).
#
# THE PAIRS MAY POINT AT THE SAME APP. The split is about ROUTES, not
# necessarily two registrations: if one app legitimately holds both scope sets,
# put its credentials in both pairs and everything works — the engine never
# compares them. Aliasing the two pairs to one app is ACCEPTED, not a defect
# — see PROJECT.md § "Credential aliasing (accepted — noted 2026-07-31)", which
# also records the two consequences that stay binding: the separated-app shape
# remains the TARGET (a read path that cannot write is a property only a separate
# registration can give you), and the CODE MUST KEEP RESOLVING THE TWO
# INDEPENDENTLY — no code may collapse them, infer one from the other, or fall
# back across them. Aliasing is a tenant configuration fact, never something the
# library relies on. So: fill in BOTH pairs. Leaving one unset in the hope the
# other covers it is the one thing that does not work.
#
# This app needs:
#   * Scope `docx:document` — create/edit new-version docs (block-level writes).
#     Without it no doc write lands. Remember: scopes take effect only after the
#     app version is RELEASED (and admin-approved where required).
#   * Scope `bitable:app` — the Base write ladder. Without it no record can be
#     created, updated or deleted.
#   * No document sharing is needed for the self-provisioning harness: it
#     CREATES its own disposable scratch docs via the API, and an app owns what
#     it creates. Writes run ONLY against such scratch docs (or the standing
#     write-scratch doc below) — never a real document — and every round-trip
#     restores byte-identical baseline.
LARK_WRITE_APP_ID=
LARK_WRITE_APP_SECRET=

# ── Standing write-scratch doc (maintainer-provided sandbox) ──────────────────────────
# A dedicated, expendable doc for write-then-read-back experiments — the whole
# doc exists so write tooling can mutate it freely without ever risking
# something real. It COMPLEMENTS (does not replace) the self-provisioning
# harness: `pytest -m live_write` still creates and destroys its OWN docs and
# never needs this URL. Like the canary/zoo URLs below, the committed URL is a
# PLACEHOLDER of the right shape (its token says Example) — put your own
# throwaway doc's URL in your .env. Unlike the harness's self-created docs, this one is maintainer-created: the WRITE app must be
# added to it as an editor collaborator before writes to it will succeed.
LARKDOCIO_WRITE_SCRATCH_DOC=https://your-domain.larksuite.com/docx/DocScratchExample0000000001

# ── Live read-canary (pytest -m live) ───────────────────────────────────────
# The disposable scratch doc the live read-canary downloads
# (tests/test_live_canary.py). This is a DISPOSABLE, API-created test doc —
# NEVER a real document. The canary is read-only (it never writes to Lark). Unset →
# the live canary skips with a clear message; plain `pytest` never needs this
# (the live tests are deselected by default).
# NOTE: the URL committed below is a PLACEHOLDER of the right shape, not a real
# document — create a throwaway doc for the canary and put its URL in your
# gitignored .env, beside the credentials (which are blank here). A value still
# on the `your-domain` host reads as UNSET, so the ring skips naming the variable
# instead of running against the placeholder.
LARKDOCIO_CANARY_DOC=https://your-domain.larksuite.com/docx/DocCanaryExample00000000001

# ── Block-zoo live ring (pytest -m zoo) — ADVISORY, never a gate ─────────────
# The disposable "block zoo" doc the advisory zoo ring reads
# (tests/test_zoo_ring.py) — a broad sampler of block types used to re-harvest
# offline fixtures each time the zoo is revised. Like the canary above it is a
# DISPOSABLE, best-effort test doc — NEVER a real document — and the ring is
# READ-ONLY. The zoo ring is ADVISORY: it is deselected by default and is NOT in
# verify.sh/CI as a gate, so it NEVER blocks anything. Unset → the ring skips
# with a clear message (never a silent pass); the binding oracle is the offline
# fixtures + goldens, not this live doc.


LARKDOCIO_BLOCKZOO_DOC=https://your-domain.larksuite.com/docx/DocBlockZooExample000000001
#
# ── OPTIONAL Drive-canary folder (pytest -m live) ────────────────────────────
# The live Drive canary — THE R1 GATE (tests/test_live_drive_canary.py, LD-328)
# — reads the BOT'S OWN root folder, discovered live, by default. It needs NO
# folder here: a Drive folder cannot be self-provisioned because create_folder is
# a PERMANENT write (no purge verb exists in drive/v1), so the canary reads a
# space the app already owns. This OPTIONAL var OVERRIDES that subject with a
# Maintainer-designated folder (a folder token or a Drive folder URL — only the trailing
# token is used). READ-ONLY. Set-but-unreachable ⇒ the canary SKIPS LOUDLY naming
# this variable (never a failure, never a silent pass); UNSET ⇒ the bot's own
# root. Leave it unset unless you have a specific folder you want the gate to pin.
# LARKDOCIO_CANARY_FOLDER=
#
# ── THE DISPOSABLES FOLDER — REQUIRED for every live test that CREATES anything ──
# The caller-supplied-destination rule: larkdocio creates nothing in a place you
# did not name, and its own live gates obey the same rule. This is where every
# disposable they make — documents, spreadsheets, Bases, folders, uploads — lands.
#
# WHY it is not optional and cannot default: with no destination Lark files the
# object in the APPLICATION'S OWN SPACE, which has no human interface at all —
# nobody can browse it (not even a full tenant admin), its trash cannot be listed
# or emptied, and a delete there TRASHES rather than purges, so what is lost there
# is lost permanently. Measured, not assumed.
#
# WHAT TO DO, once:
#   1. Create a folder in YOUR OWN Drive (any name; "larkdocio disposables" is fine).
#   2. In the Lark console, GRANT THE APPLICATION ACCESS TO THAT FOLDER. The library
#      never widens, requests or escalates its own access — a grant is a console
#      action, and this is the only one this variable needs.
#   3. Paste the folder's LINK or its TOKEN below (only the trailing path segment of
#      a link is used, so either form works).
# Then everything the live gates create is visible to you and deletable by you.
#
# UNSET ⇒ every live test that creates something SKIPS LOUDLY naming this variable.
# Never a silent pass, and never a fall-back to the application's own space.
# The offline suite is unaffected (the live rings are deselected by default).
# LARKDOCIO_DISPOSABLES_FOLDER=
#
# Optional companion for the wiki-only block types (42 wiki_catalog / 51
# sub_page_list): the disposable zoo WIKI page, if one is added. Unset → those
# types simply stay unevidenced; the core zoo ring does not need it.
# LARKDOCIO_BLOCKZOO_WIKI=

# ── Scratch surfaces for the beasts (Sheets / Base tranches) ─────────────────
# Maintainer-provided standing sandboxes (2026-07-26) for the Sheets and Base work —
# disposable, NEVER real data, read via the READ app (reachability verified:
# spreadsheet metadata, tab query, and bitable app-get all return code 0 with
# read creds; no extra share was needed). Like the scratch/canary/zoo URLs
# above, the committed URLs are PLACEHOLDERS of the right shape — put your own
# sandboxes' URLs in your .env. LARKDOCIO_SCRATCH_SHEET is a READ-ONLY target — the sheets
# canary never writes. LARKDOCIO_SCRATCH_BASE is NOT read-only: read the
# free-fire paragraph below AND the warning that follows it before you touch it.
#
# LARKDOCIO_SCRATCH_SHEET is CONSUMED by the L1 live sheets canary — THE L1 gate
# (tests/test_live_canary.py, `pytest -m live`, LD-106): two back-to-back
# read-only renders through the L1 values→Markdown path must be byte-identical
# (determinism). Unset → the sheets canary skips loudly (never a silent pass);
# plain `pytest` never needs it (live is deselected by default).
#
# LARKDOCIO_SCRATCH_BASE is the maintainer-GRANTED Scratch Base (2026-08-03): the free-fire
# zone for Base development — writable at will, affects no one (the one explicit
# exception to the never-touch-a-real-document rule; every other Base stays
# untouchable). CONSUMED BY A SHIPPED GATE since LD-252:
# tests/test_live_base_canary.py is THE L1 GATE for the Base surface (`pytest -m live`)
# and reads this variable twice in one run; tests/test_live_base_read_arm.py and
# tests/test_live_base_destructive.py both skip loudly without it. The
# Base study probes (2026-08-03; captures in tests/captures/base-study/) were its first users.
#
# WHAT YOU CANNOT PUT BACK — read this before you act on that grant. This Base
# carries three column kinds Lark's field API CANNOT CREATE BY ANY REQUEST:
# `lookup`, `Email` and `stage`. If you delete one, NO CODE IN THIS REPOSITORY can
# put it back — the only route to a Base carrying one is copying a Base that
# already has one (`POST …/apps/{a}/copy`), the route the write harness sanctions
# and the reason it says so (tests/base_write_harness.py:126-137: "the only
# hand-authored Lookup, Email and Stage fields in existence — un-authorable types
# the API *cannot* recreate"). Concretely, deleting the `lookup` column turns two
# live arms PERMANENTLY red: tests/test_live_base_computed_provenance.py:209 and
# tests/test_live_base_destructive.py:398 each assert that column exists, so that
# the arm cannot pass vacuously. Records and anything you add yourself are yours;
# the hand-authored columns are the part nobody can re-make.
LARKDOCIO_SCRATCH_SHEET=https://your-domain.larksuite.com/sheets/SheetScratchExample00000001
LARKDOCIO_SCRATCH_BASE=https://your-domain.larksuite.com/base/BaseScratchExample000000001

# ── The COMMENT ZOO surfaces (maintainer-granted 2026-07-29; LD-161) ─────────────────
# Two maintainer-provided throwaway sandboxes carrying HAND-MADE **anchored** comment
# examples — Simple / Resolved / Thread-with-replies / With-a-Like / Edited /
# With-Image-Embedded — one set on each surface. They exist because the API
# **cannot create an anchored comment at all** (`dev/comments-probe-2026-07-29.md`
# § Q1a: 18 candidate routes absent; the one create route silently produces a
# whole-document comment), so these are the only real inline-`comment_ids`
# payloads obtainable, and they were made by hand in the UI.
#
# NOT consumed by any test or gate — they are a **standing fixture source** read
# by the maintainers' probes, whose captures — kept here as
# `tests/captures/comments-probe-round2-captures/` — are the durable artifact.
# Unset → those probes refuse to run with a clear message (never a silent pass);
# plain `pytest` never needs either URL.
#
# **Posture (maintainer grant, LD-161):** COMMENT-level operations are granted on both;
# every other operation is READ-ONLY, save the one plan-gated sub-span edit
# LD-161 AC6 runs and restores byte-identically. The zoo is hand-made and
# irreplaceable — read-capture before you touch anything. Like the canary/zoo/
# scratch URLs above, both committed URLs are PLACEHOLDERS of the right shape —
# put your own zoo's URLs in your .env.
LARKDOCIO_COMMENT_ZOO_DOC=https://your-domain.larksuite.com/docx/DocCommentZooExample0000001
LARKDOCIO_COMMENT_ZOO_SHEET=https://your-domain.larksuite.com/sheets/SheetCommentZooExample00001

# ── The MERMAID SPECIMEN doc (maintainer-provided 2026-07-29; LD-162) ────────────────
# The document carrying a real, hand-typed `/mermaid` block — an Add-Ons block
# (docx Block Type 40). It is the ONLY way to see the exact `add_ons` payload
# **Lark itself** produces for a Mermaid block, which is what LD-162's gate spike
# replays. It happens to be the SAME document as LARKDOCIO_COMMENT_ZOO_DOC above
# (the maintainer put the specimen at the bottom of it), but it is named separately
# because the GRANT IS DIFFERENT and the two must not be conflated:
#
# **Posture (maintainer grant, LD-162): READ-ONLY, no exceptions.** The comment-zoo grant
# on the same token covers COMMENT operations; it does not extend to block
# creates or content writes for the mermaid work. Every LD-162 create attempt
# runs on a disposable the harness provisions and deletes inside the same run —
# the probe library's disposable check refuses any other token by name,
# including this one.
#
# NOT consumed by any test or gate — read only by the maintainers' probes, whose
# captures — kept here as `tests/captures/mermaid-gate-captures/` — are the
# durable artifact. Unset → those probes refuse to run with a clear message
# (never a silent pass); plain `pytest` never needs it.
LARKDOCIO_MERMAID_SPECIMEN_DOC=https://your-domain.larksuite.com/docx/DocCommentZooExample0000001

# ── The account that should be able to SEE what the application created ─────
# The email address of the person who owns this tenant's larkdocio setup — the
# human whose Lark account should be granted access to objects the APPLICATION
# created. It exists because an application identity owns a Drive space of its
# own, and that space is not listed in any human's Drive UI: objects created
# without a destination land there, and nobody can open the folder, browse it,
# or empty its trash from the product.
#
# USED ONLY to grant a named human access to objects the application created —
# never to look anybody up, never written into a document, never sent anywhere
# else. It must be an email address or an `open_id`; a bare display name is
# refused before any call is made.
#
# UNSET ⇒ any run that needs it REFUSES with a message naming this variable —
# never a silent pass, and never an address inferred from your git config, a
# commit trailer, or any other source. Set it before such a run.
#
# Nothing in the shipped library reads this, and no gate needs it: plain
# `pytest` and every normal download never touch it.
# LARKDOCIO_GRANT_EMAIL=

# ── The MEDIA-MINTING OPT-IN (LD-505) ───────────────────────────────────────
# NOT a credential and NOT a target: this names the ISSUE that authorized a run
# to spend permanent residue. Eight live files upload bytes, and an upload mints
# a drive media token that NOTHING removes — no route in Lark's open API purges
# drive media (measured, ruled D-95 in requirements/DECISIONS.md — in the dev
# repo, not in this one), and deleting
# the document, table or workbook does not take the bytes with it. So those eight
# rings are LOUD-SKIPPED by default, even under their own marker.
#
# The VALUE is the authorizing issue id, never a truthy flag — so the answer to
# "who said yes?" travels in the run's own transcript instead of a shell history:
#
#   LARKDOCIO_MEDIA_MINT_AUTHORIZED_BY=LD-507 pytest -m live_write
#
# Unset ⇒ loud skip. Set to anything that is not an LD-NNN id ⇒ loud skip saying
# so. Never a silent run either way. The echo is a RECORDED CLAIM, not a
# permission check: no allow-list of ids exists in this repo, the authority is
# the maintainer's declared budget in that issue's own file, and QA reads both. Before
# setting it, check the maintainers' media-token ledger (dev/media-token-ledger.md,
# in the dev repo) — reuse beats mint.
#
# Plain `pytest` never needs it: the rings are deselected by default and the
# guardrail is inert on that selection.
# LARKDOCIO_MEDIA_MINT_AUTHORIZED_BY=

# ── The LIVE-WRITE CONSENT OPT-IN (LD-537) ──────────────────────────────────
# NOT a credential and NOT a target: this names the ISSUE that authorized a run
# to fire the WRITE ring. Those gates self-provision REAL objects in the live
# tenant — documents, Bases, spreadsheets, folders — and eight of their files
# also upload bytes nothing can purge (see the block above).
#
# WHY A SECOND FENCE EXISTS. `addopts` in pyproject.toml already DESELECTS the
# ring, and that stays. But a deselection is a SELECTION control, and one typed
# `-o addopts=""` on a run that executes tests clears it — and that flag has now
# been typed twice: wave 0 (~3 permanent media tokens, ids lost) and 2026-08-20
# (15 disposable Bases created in under three minutes; 14 self-deleted, and the
# 15th — BaseGoneExample000000000001 — read as GONE BY THE ORACLE on 2026-08-25:
# the bitable route no longer resolves it, while its ledger row still reads
# deleted: false. Whether a human reclaimed it or it aged past the lag horizon is
# NOT ESTABLISHED by that reading). So SELECTION and CONSENT are asked separately now: clearing the
# deselection RE-SELECTS the ring but does NOT ARM it.
#
# The VALUE is the authorizing issue id, never a truthy flag — which is the
# fence's teeth, because both incidents were a typed flag:
#
#   LARKDOCIO_LIVE_WRITE_AUTHORIZED_BY=LD-537 pytest -m live_write
#
# Unset ⇒ loud skip naming this variable. Set to anything that is not an LD-NNN
# id ⇒ loud skip saying so. Never a silent run either way. The echo is a
# RECORDED CLAIM, not a permission check: no allow-list of ids exists in this
# repo, the authority is the maintainer's word in that issue's own file, and QA reads
# both. AFTER an armed run, check what it left behind — .base-disposables.json
# and tests/base_write_harness.py's orphans() — and reclaim any survivor.
#
# LEFT COMMENTED OUT DELIBERATELY. setup.sh seeds .env from this file on a fresh
# clone, so an uncommented line here would arm the ring on every new worktree —
# a consent fence a bootstrap script arms is not a fence. Plain `pytest` never
# needs it: the ring is deselected by default and the fence is inert there.
# LARKDOCIO_LIVE_WRITE_AUTHORIZED_BY=

# ── Optional knobs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lark's IP allowlist is IPv4-only, so the CLI pins DNS to IPv4 by default.
# Set to 1/true/yes to restore stock resolution (e.g. on an IPv6-only host).
# LARK_DISABLE_IPV4_PIN=

# Logging verbosity: INFO (default) or DEBUG (adds a [larkdocio] prefix).
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ── Live capture output (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# An authorised live-write run RECORDS what it observed, and those recordings are
# evidence: guards pin them, closed work cites them, and this project's rule that
# nothing is verified by a success code rests on them. So a run is NOT allowed to
# write inside a git working tree — it would overwrite a committed capture, which
# happened four times before the fence existed, once laundering a refusal into a
# success while the file's own note still reasoned from the refusal.
#
# Captures therefore land in a RUN-SCOPED directory outside every working tree, and
# each redirect is announced with the path it used. To keep one deliberately, copy it
# back and commit it yourself — authoring evidence is a human act, not a side effect.
# LARKDOCIO_LIVE_CAPTURE_DIR=  # unset ⇒ a per-run temp dir; set to pin the run root

# ── Real identities for the live contacts / access arms (LD-684) ─────────────
# The repository carries only FAKE people and ids (`ou_exampleowner…`,
# `example.user@example.com`). The live arms that must address a REAL person
# read that person from here and SKIP LOUDLY when the variable is unset. Never
# commit a value: these are your tenant's identifiers.
#   LARKDOCIO_LIVE_OWNER_OPEN_ID     — the account owner's open_id
#   LARKDOCIO_LIVE_OWNER_EMAIL       — the owner's address (resolve_colleague)
#   LARKDOCIO_LIVE_CONTROL_OPEN_ID   — another user's open_id, whose display name
#                                      batch_get_user_names returns (the
#                                      name search itself reaches at most the
#                                      owner — larkdocio.contacts says why)
#   LD348_COLLEAGUE_EMAIL            — another user's address the live
#                                      resolve_colleague gate looks up
#   LARKDOCIO_LIVE_COLLEAGUE_OPEN_ID — the user LD348_COLLEAGUE_EMAIL resolves to
#   LARKDOCIO_LIVE_GRANTEE_EMAIL     — the human the live Drive access gate grants to
# LARKDOCIO_LIVE_OWNER_OPEN_ID=
# LARKDOCIO_LIVE_OWNER_EMAIL=
# LARKDOCIO_LIVE_CONTROL_OPEN_ID=
# LD348_COLLEAGUE_EMAIL=
# LARKDOCIO_LIVE_COLLEAGUE_OPEN_ID=
# LARKDOCIO_LIVE_GRANTEE_EMAIL=
